;; A fixed-length dataset of one record type whose SYNCHRONIZED items leave
;; bytes no item covers. Nothing in this file says so: SYNCHRONIZED is a
;; copybook clause, and where the slack falls is the compiler's answer rather
;; than a statement a layout carries. What the layout does say is `lrecl 20`,
;; and that belongs to this entry's *producer* claim rather than to the run a
;; generator is put through: every record type MUST account for all of LRECL, so
;; a reading that dropped a run would leave that run's bytes with no place in
;; this dataset -- one of them, or three, or four if it dropped both. Nothing
;; downstream re-checks it, because a record length is not something a
;; descriptor carries: `ir.json` says `unframed` and no number beside it.
;;
;; The framing is the smallest there is, which is deliberate. Under `recfm F` a
;; record's bytes are its extent and the next record begins at the byte after
;; it, so nothing in the file agrees or disagrees about where a record ends: a
;; consumer that wrote a record back without its slack would put the second
;; record four bytes early, and the round trip would read it as something else.
(encoding
  (charset ascii)
  (sign-convention ascii-zone-37)
  (byte-order big-endian)
  (float-format ieee-754))

(framing
  (recfm F)
  (lrecl 20))

(record SYNC
  (copybook "sync.cpy" SYNC-RECORD))

(discriminate SYNC single-record-type)

(sequence (* SYNC))
